WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.480
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.780
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.060
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.320
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.140
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:20.120
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.300
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.740
Oh, we have a really amazing guest on Office Ladies today.

9
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.000
We sure do.

10
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.840
All of you that listened to Work Bus last week know that today in the studio, we have Bryan Cranston.

11
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:48.900
We cannot wait for you guys to hear this interview because, look, there are certain episodes of The Office that are just real big episodes, and they warrant extra time.

12
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.520
And Work Bus is definitely one of those.

13
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:52.860
It's definitely one of those.

14
00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.060
Yeah, and we shared all about our experience on that bus, but we really felt like you needed to hear from the director as well.

15
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.020
So we reached out to Bryan.

16
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.280
He was so kind and gracious.

17
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.360
He came into the studio and chatted all things Work Bus with us.

18
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.220
And more.

19
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.440
Now, I know a lot of people know Bryan Cranston from his many acting roles.

20
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.460
He's been in film and television, and he's been on Broadway.

21
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:24.400
You know, there's Malcolm in the Middle and Walter White on Breaking Bad, and he even won a Tony Award for the Broadway play all the way.

22
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:25.880
He's amazing.

23
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.020
But Bryan is also a very accomplished director.

24
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:37.940
He directed episodes of Malcolm in the Middle, of Breaking Bad, Modern Family, Sneaky Pete, and his new series, Your Honor, on Showtime.

25
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.160
And, of course, this very epic, famous episode of The Office.

26
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.780
And he is just delightful.

27
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.000
He is so much fun to talk to.

28
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.780
It was such a joy having him.

29
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.860
And, Jenna, you know what I was thinking?

30
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.320
We didn't bring this up to him, but I want him to direct our road trip movie, if we ever do it.

31
00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.840
Oh, my gosh.

32
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:00.520
Yes.

33
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:01.260
Yes.

34
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.520
We have never even shared.

35
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.240
Have we shared about the fact that me, you, and Lee have a road trip idea?

36
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.020
We came up with an idea for a road trip movie.

37
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.400
Lee wrote it.

38
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.160
It's so funny.

39
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.560
It's so funny.

40
00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:15.500
I know.

41
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:21.980
Some of the hardest I have laughed in a long time was when the three of us were sitting at your dining table and we were pitching jokes.

42
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.340
Because, listen, you guys, this is a road trip movie about two gals that are, like, in their 50s.

43
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:28.740
Yes.

44
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.300
But, anyway, I mean.

45
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.640
It has a mom detective's element to it, I have to say.

46
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:34.840
It really does.

47
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:35.480
Yeah.

48
00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.480
But would Bryan Cranston ever direct anything on the road again?

49
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.540
That's the question.

50
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.800
I think after you listen to this interview, you might find that he would be very hesitant to do so.

51
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:54.520
Well, listen, the office might have ruined him forever in directing anything in a moving vehicle again.

52
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.980
But let's let him tell you about it.

53
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.460
We're going to take a break.

54
00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:05.460
And when we come back, our interview with Bryan Cranston about Work Bus, which is also known as Death Bus.

55
00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:06.580
We'll be right back.

56
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.420
It's such a great interview.

57
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:08.420
We'll be right back.

58
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:20.720
Yeah.

59
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:21.580
Yeah.

60
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:22.080
Woo!

61
00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.160
See, well, who wrote your opening theme song?

62
00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:26.320
Creed Bratton.

63
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.020
Did he really?

64
00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:27.300
Yes.

65
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.380
You know, I just realized who Creed was.

66
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:31.900
You just did.

67
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.720
I just, in the, like, in the last few weeks.

68
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.220
Grassroots, all of that.

69
00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.600
Yes, the grassroots.

70
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.080
And I'm thinking, that can't be the same.

71
00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.180
It's the same guy.

72
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:42.480
Yes.

73
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.160
Now, my question to you, my first question.

74
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.480
Thanks for coming on the show, by the way.

75
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:56.560
My first question to you would be, did the show ever do a backstage idea about what Creed's background was?

76
00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.660
Did someone ever go, that guy looks so familiar?

77
00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.100
And, you know, did you ever do anything like that?

78
00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:03.880
I don't remember.

79
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.340
There were so many pitches.

80
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:16.220
There were so many people who wanted to do a whole episode just on Creed and his life and wanted him to be outed as Creed Bratton, Creed Bratton,

81
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.300
because he's the only person on the show who has his full name as his character name.

82
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.860
Like, Angela is Angela on the show, but on the show her last name is Martin.

83
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.540
But Creed Bratton, the character, is also Creed Bratton, the man.

84
00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.620
But I don't think it was ever revealed.

85
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.140
I will say, though, of all the characters, we have a show Bible.

86
00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:35.780
Sorry.

87
00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:36.860
I have to.

88
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.920
You guys, Brian, his microphone's this morning.

89
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:43.780
Something's up with it.

90
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.760
And there is a bar in front of his face.

91
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.020
And so for me to talk to Brian, he has to look.

92
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.840
Angela, it's safer to have a bar in front of my face.

93
00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.100
It helps the restraining order issue that we have.

94
00:04:55.100 --> 00:05:01.860
But anyway, of all the characters, we had a show Bible of all their back history.

95
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.240
And Creed's is the crazieSt.

96
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.820
It's the most fun to read.

97
00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.060
He has like a family in Canada.

98
00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.300
He lives under his desk half the time.

99
00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.340
It's like all over the place.

100
00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:12.440
Now, wait a minute.

101
00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.660
Why didn't I get a chance to read that?

102
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.160
The show Bible.

103
00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.460
They didn't give you the show Bible?

104
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.480
No, I didn't get it.

105
00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.400
No one said it was available.

106
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:21.520
You should have been given that.

107
00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.920
I absolutely should have been given that.

108
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.200
Would have changed WorkBus.

109
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:25.360
I'm going to quit.

110
00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:28.420
Retroactive.

111
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.820
Well, we always like to ask people when they come on, first question, how did you end up working on The Office?

112
00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:35.420
Oh.

113
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.680
How'd you get this job directing WorkBus?

114
00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.220
Tracy Katsky is a friend of mine.

115
00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:47.600
And she was working as a consultant with David in the last year you guys were on the air.

116
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:48.080
Okay.

117
00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:48.380
Right?

118
00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:56.860
And I had just finished my season with Breaking Bad, and that was 2012.

119
00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:57.520
Yep.

120
00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.180
I think.

121
00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.640
It was 2012.

122
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:05.240
And Tracy calls and says, we're looking for directors for this new season.

123
00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.380
Would you be interested in coming on board The Office?

124
00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.120
And immediately the answer was going to be, of course, no.

125
00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.740
But I love the show.

126
00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:21.800
It's such a legendary show that any chance to be a part of it in some small way.

127
00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:30.820
And she said, I know you've been directing Breaking Bad's, and you know, you're out in the desert, and it's hot, and it's sticky.

128
00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:37.060
How would you like to come to, what was it, Burbank, North Hollywood, wherever we were?

129
00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.020
And we work inside.

130
00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:40.480
Yeah.

131
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:41.300
A little off comedy.

132
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.000
It's a short day.

133
00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:42.860
Air-conditioned.

134
00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:47.260
Air-conditioned, easy, indoors, five days, bang, bang, bang.

135
00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:47.840
Boom, boom, boom.

136
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.300
And I said, oh, it sounds fantastic.

137
00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:51.900
Let's do it.

138
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.000
And she said, well, we have such and such slot open.

139
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.800
And I looked at my calendar, and I said, I'll take that slot.

140
00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:58.180
Yeah.

141
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:05.820
And I don't know, you probably described it on your show before, but the way it works in television for directors is by time.

142
00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:13.680
You have no idea when you sign on to a show what your episode is going to be by the time it gets to your time slot.

143
00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.540
So we had no idea.

144
00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:25.660
And it just so happened that Work Bus came onto my lap.

145
00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:29.100
What did you think when you read this episode?

146
00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:39.860
Well, the first, I was excited to read it, read it right away, and Work Bus, oh, I don't know, oh, they exterminate, was it exterminating?

147
00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.360
No, we were exterminating, this was 11 years ago, I think.

148
00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:49.800
There was this electrical issue, electromagnetic things in the walls, and Dwight was going to have to open up all the walls and redo the electrical.

149
00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:59.060
So the basic idea is that there could not be a work stoppage for the whole team.

150
00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:12.560
So a work bus was found to be the best method to continue working while this work on the actual location, the office, was taking place.

151
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:21.120
So everybody had to load onto the bus, desks were on there, as we all know, and we had to just keep traveling.

152
00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.540
We're in the San Fernando Valley in AuguSt.

153
00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.360
It's so hot in AuguSt.

154
00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:26.760
It's so hot.

155
00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:30.180
I went to the call sheets, it was 100 degrees this week.

156
00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:31.280
It was like triple digits.

157
00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:32.560
It was very hot.

158
00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:40.300
So the first thing I did was curse my friend Tracy Katzky and say, damn you, as if they knew, you know.

159
00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.820
But it was a challenge.

160
00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:48.420
I think we shot in on the set for a day and a half or something like that.

161
00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:48.900
Yeah, yeah.

162
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:49.180
Something short.

163
00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.120
And then the rest of the time we were on that bus or in the park.

164
00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.360
And then we were at a baseball field, I know.

165
00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:57.220
Yep, right.

166
00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:58.820
We had that cold open, yeah.

167
00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.820
I enjoyed it for the most part.

168
00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.860
There were some tough moments.

169
00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:13.340
Well, I have to say, okay, you know that our cast has renamed this episode Death Bus.

170
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.280
This has come to you, I know.

171
00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:20.600
And Jenna and I wrote a whole chapter in our book about it, The Office PFFs.

172
00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.180
We have a copy for you.

173
00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:22.620
We signed.

174
00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:23.240
Oh, I love it.

175
00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.680
Inside it says, Brian, we owe you our lives.

176
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:27.560
Thanks, Angela and Jenna.

177
00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.020
It is chapter 12.

178
00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:35.360
And we dedicated a whole chapter to just this episode.

179
00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:38.240
And we have fantastic photos of you.

180
00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:42.840
But we realized after our book came out, you were doing press.

181
00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:49.660
We felt really bad because every single producer clearly wanted you to be asked about Death Bus.

182
00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.540
Jenna was like, oh, no, Ange.

183
00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:53.220
Poor Brian.

184
00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:58.360
We wrote about this and now everyone is asking him the same question on every talk show.

185
00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.460
Why did you try to kill the cast of The Office?

186
00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.100
Why did I try to kill the cast?

187
00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.500
Was it a murder for hire?

188
00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:05.600
No one's asked.

189
00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.100
Was it a failed murder for hire?

190
00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.280
You can't talk about it.

191
00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:09.920
I can't discuss all those details.

192
00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:14.480
There is a current investigation going on.

193
00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:14.900
Ongoing.

194
00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:15.600
Ongoing.

195
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:19.040
And, you know, indictments will be handed down.

196
00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.660
I have every reason to believe you two are in the clear.

197
00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.020
I can't be positive on that.

198
00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:29.540
Set of circumstances.

199
00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.580
It was hot and tight.

200
00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:36.740
You know, you talk about the set being contained.

201
00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.860
Well, we're on a bus.

202
00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:39.460
Yeah.

203
00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:41.180
And we didn't have a lot of room.

204
00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:46.680
And if I recall, nothing was actually nailed down.

205
00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.260
Everything was kind of shifting around.

206
00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.940
Desks were actually shifting and moving.

207
00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.800
And I'm looking at this going, oh, my God.

208
00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:04.680
I think people didn't realize this bus is actually on the road, on an open street and moving.

209
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.720
Well, at first there was comedy in that, right?

210
00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:12.860
Like, at first, we're traveling down the road and, like, our little coffee cup is moving across the desk.

211
00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:13.720
Our papers.

212
00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.600
We're holding on to things.

213
00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:15.860
Yeah.

214
00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.420
We hit a pothole.

215
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:18.880
My lamp slid in my lap.

216
00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:19.640
I caught it.

217
00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.120
Yay.

218
00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:21.060
That's funny.

219
00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:21.600
It's funny.

220
00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:30.740
But then there was a part of the script where, I guess, Dwight swerves and we all kind of slide and we all are like, ah.

221
00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.760
Yeah, he's trying to get us to the pie.

222
00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:33.920
Yeah, get to the pie.

223
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:40.740
And I guess the AD yelled to the cast, we weren't really swerving, lean right.

224
00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.280
And we kind of, ah, and no one bought it.

225
00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:44.260
It didn't look good.

226
00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:45.340
And so then.

227
00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:46.760
They unloaded rain.

228
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.760
Rain left and a stunt driver got on the bus.

229
00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:50.600
Right.

230
00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:57.760
They removed us from the tow rig and now we are truly just a bus traveling down the road with a stunt driver.

231
00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.840
We listed what all was in the bus.

232
00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.740
Like we went back and screen grabbed it.

233
00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:14.480
It was a full like Arrowhead water jug dispenser, a microwave, a coffee pot, lamps, rolling chairs, desks, computers, a printer.

234
00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.920
Was there a little mini Xerox machine?

235
00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:18.320
I bet there was.

236
00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:26.140
So it was a full office and it all, when the stunt driver swerved right, it all swerved.

237
00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:27.220
Everything moved.

238
00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.300
And all of a sudden, that was not part of the production discussion that we had, by the way.

239
00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:32.840
I'm thinking about that.

240
00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:40.500
It was like, we're doing this, rain's going to drive and we're going to do this and it's all contained and we'll have the cast move back and forth.

241
00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:41.980
And it didn't look good.

242
00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:43.560
Everybody was in sync.

243
00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.400
And there was something funny about things happening at the same time and once.

244
00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.480
And you can't duplicate that.

245
00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:51.220
You can't fake that.

246
00:12:51.340 --> 00:13:05.740
And so there was an idea, and I don't believe it was my idea, but I'm not saying it's not, to actually heighten the movement of the thing with a professional stunt driver.

247
