515
00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.980
Well, Leslie, I remember looking at Leslie David Baker and his eyes were bloodshot and watering.

516
00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:37.340
And I kept thinking, like, maybe the vent is hitting him at an angle.

517
00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:39.960
You know how it dries out your eyes, like if a vent hits you.

518
00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:42.080
But no, it was just poison.

519
00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:44.820
Now, was Leslie's desk close to yours?

520
00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:45.300
Yes.

521
00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:46.040
I'm trying to remember.

522
00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:53.620
When we had the, when we all went flying into one another, I believe I flew into Leslie.

523
00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:58.320
Did Leslie, when we got off, I remember he was like, I'm not getting back on.

524
00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:00.800
Leslie said, I'm never getting back on the bus again.

525
00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:01.340
I'm done.

526
00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:02.340
I'm done getting on the bus.

527
00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:06.640
I remember him walking to his car and driving away, but I think that might not be true.

528
00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:09.720
But for some reason, I remember Leslie was like, goodbye.

529
00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:10.720
I'm out.

530
00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:11.300
I'm out.

531
00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:12.120
Shoot around me.

532
00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:16.500
But also, poor Leslie, whenever we went on locations, he got the worst of it.

533
00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:21.760
When we went to the beach, he got sand kicked in his eye and it scratched his cornea.

534
00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:24.140
And he had to wear, like, a patch for a week.

535
00:23:24.220 --> 00:23:24.380
Yeah.

536
00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:25.100
Oof.

537
00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:28.980
So, I think Leslie was like, I don't do scenes outside of the office anymore.

538
00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:29.680
Forget it.

539
00:23:29.780 --> 00:23:30.400
Forget it.

540
00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:32.220
I don't go on location.

541
00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:33.260
Shit goes down.

542
00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:34.960
It almost sounds like his character.

543
00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:36.060
You just drill.

544
00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.800
I don't go outside.

545
00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:38.340
Yeah.

546
00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:38.840
Exactly.

547
00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:39.800
Don't leave the office.

548
00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:45.280
Were there any moments that you haven't talked about about this episode?

549
00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:48.340
Because we know you've been hit up with those questions quite a bit.

550
00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:52.040
But any behind-the-scenes memories that you have that you'd want to share?

551
00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:52.720
Yeah.

552
00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:59.800
There was – those things are so on the frontal lobe that it was – it's hard to remember.

553
00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.320
It was just – I just had a really good time other than worrying about that.

554
00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:13.460
I was really happy to be able to be on your set and see it and feel it.

555
00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:20.240
And you guys – by the time I came along, it was the ninth season, so you were a well-oiled machine.

556
00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:31.400
And the role of a guest director is not to come in and – I wouldn't dare, wouldn't dare say anything to an actor about their character.

557
00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:34.720
How – wouldn't that be obnoxious?

558
00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.280
It's like season nine.

559
00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:38.700
I think you could make a different choice for Dwight.

560
00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:41.560
I think – let's make this – I think he's friendlier.

561
00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:42.060
Yeah.

562
00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:45.100
Why is Angela such a bitch?

563
00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:47.620
What's with the cats?

564
00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:48.980
Let's do away with – let's forget all that.

565
00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:49.480
No more cats.

566
00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:50.080
No more cats.

567
00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:51.820
Yeah.

568
00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:54.540
And then you two, you know, I'd say I'd pull you apart.

569
00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:57.780
Start to think about something about Jim that you didn't like.

570
00:24:58.520 --> 00:24:58.760
Yeah.

571
00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:01.280
Just throw a wrench into everything.

572
00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.640
He's like, I don't think this – so there's nothing – a director.

573
00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:13.720
A director on episodic television just comes in and tries to – what my whole goal was whenever I direct television,

574
00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:22.700
which is different from directing features because in features it's – you are trying and need to please yourself.

575
00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:29.140
And make sure that you get all those elements and moments that you've worked so hard in breaking down that script.

576
00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:39.100
In television, it's about staying true to these characters, putting them in positions where they can really be themselves, where they feel comfortable.

577
00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:45.280
It's delivering the meat and potatoes that every showrunner is going to expect from you.

578
00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:55.340
I always thought if I can deliver two or three things that the showrunner didn't see, that's golden.

579
00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:58.120
Just a couple little things.

580
00:25:58.120 --> 00:25:59.760
I think there's a few.

581
00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:05.680
I mean, when I rewatched the episode, I completely forgot that Ed plays the banjo at the end.

582
00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:06.600
Oh, yeah, yeah.

583
00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:08.860
I mean, and just rips it.

584
00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:10.040
It's just so great.

585
00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:12.140
And everyone is just like, oh, whatever.

586
00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.540
Well, no, and it's true.

587
00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:16.440
I taught him the banjo in two weeks.

588
00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:20.540
And he was able to pick it up and perform that.

589
00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:20.960
That's amazing.

590
00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:21.320
Yeah.

591
00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.280
I loved all the pie scenes, too.

592
00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:24.460
I thought they were really sweet.

593
00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:25.800
Did you eat any pie?

594
00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:26.660
I had some pie.

595
00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:28.520
Do you remember your flavor of pie?

596
00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:30.260
That's probably hard to remember.

597
00:26:30.580 --> 00:26:32.000
I'm thinking gooseberry.

598
00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:33.060
Yeah.

599
00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:33.900
Yeah.

600
00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:34.960
That was.

601
00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:36.340
Look how happy you were.

602
00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:37.520
Well, he remembered that.

603
00:26:37.660 --> 00:26:38.500
Wasn't that one?

604
00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:39.180
Yeah.

605
00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:41.140
Because it's such a funny name.

606
00:26:41.140 --> 00:26:41.700
I like it.

607
00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:42.380
Yeah.

608
00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:47.340
There was like a, it was like a, I think it was gooseberry because it wasn't blackberry.

609
00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:47.800
No.

610
00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:50.740
It was, yeah, but there was a special berry crumble pie.

611
00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:56.240
When I rewatched it, I just remember how happy we actually all were to sit outside and have

612
00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:56.640
pie.

613
00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:57.760
Yeah.

614
00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:58.940
I had chocolate cream.

615
00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:00.900
It was delicious.

616
00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.680
Interesting choice on a very hot day.

617
00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:03.030
Huh?

618
00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:03.600
What?

619
00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:04.780
Chocolate cream.

620
00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:06.880
Peel back the layers.

621
00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:18.120
Okay.

622
00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:19.540
I have got to do something.

623
00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:24.160
I have been going through the call sheets and on the back of the call sheet, they do a

624
00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:26.000
thing called get to know your cast and crew.

625
00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:30.320
And they ask a few questions, but they're so interesting.

626
00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:34.260
You mean on every one of your call sheets, it was get to know your cast and crew?

627
00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:36.880
Someone was featured on each call sheet.

628
00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:42.660
We had talked about, Jen and I wanted to start with each new guest, ask them a few of these

629
00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:43.140
questions.

630
00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:46.580
What was one of the first jobs you had in entertainment?

631
00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:53.160
One of the first jobs I had in entertainment, I think it was a soap opera.

632
00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:58.100
I did like a three-day role on Days of Our Lives.

633
00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:00.100
I love Days of Our Lives.

634
00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:03.320
Back in 1981.

635
00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.120
What was your character?

636
00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.180
I was a cousin of the person.

637
00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:10.680
I will tell you this.

638
00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:16.860
Bill and Susan Seaforth, he and his wife, Susan and Bill, they were on Days of Our Lives

639
00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:17.400
for years.

640
00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:23.000
I happened to oversleep that morning the first day that I was to work.

641
00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:27.080
And I missed the rehearsal, missed the blocking, missed everything.

642
00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:28.600
I'm panicking.

643
00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:29.200
Yeah.

644
00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:30.520
Absolutely panicking.

645
00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:36.520
And I race to NBC where we're shooting it and I'm panicking, I'm nervous.

646
00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:41.460
And all of a sudden I hear a knocking on my dressing room door and it's Bill Hayes,

647
00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:42.840
the big star of the show.

648
00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:43.120
Yeah.

649
00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:46.860
And he says, I understand you had a problem this morning.

650
00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:49.680
We stood in for you and we blocked it.

651
00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:52.740
And he says, can I run down what we did?

652
00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:56.020
And do you need some help on the backstory of the character?

653
00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:57.980
I was so appreciative.

654
00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:00.060
I was just a day player.

655
00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:02.820
And he put himself out there to say, look.

656
00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:03.380
That is so kind.

657
00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:03.680
Yeah.

658
00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:05.380
He was a lovely, lovely guy.

659
00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:13.280
And he helped me get over the anxiety of being late and the responsibility of that.

660
00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:20.640
And I spent all the money that I made on that episode to buy flowers for the people that I

661
00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:24.620
knew stood up for me and Bill and the casting director.

662
00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:26.120
And it was like, I'm so sorry.

663
00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.780
But that was my, yeah, it was my first experience.

664
00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:29.700
Okay.

665
00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:30.700
I love that.

666
00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:31.780
I love that story.

667
00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:32.740
I love the kindness you were shown.

668
00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:35.380
I didn't know where that was going when you said he knocked on the door.

669
00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:36.140
I was like, oh, no.

670
00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:36.660
Yeah, no.

671
00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:37.800
In a good way.

672
00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:38.560
In a good way.

673
00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:39.360
Okay.

674
00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:43.180
Favorite place in the whole wide world that you've ever been to?

675
00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:44.960
I guess it would be Venice.

676
00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:51.280
I was there on my honeymoon almost 35 years ago.

677
00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:53.620
Very romantic and very unique.

678
00:29:53.620 --> 00:30:02.400
And we just had, there was one night when my wife and I were, there was a thunderous rainstorm

679
00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:11.160
and we went to a Vivaldi concert in a church that had windows at the top of this sphere

680
00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:19.360
and this dome and it felt like God was adding the exclamation to it.

681
00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:20.500
Da, da, da, da, da, da, da.

682
00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:23.540
You know, and oh my God.

683
00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:28.220
It was just so, it was just so magical and romantic.

684
00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:29.520
Unbelievable time.

685
00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:30.240
Wow.

686
00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:32.200
Have you been back since your honeymoon?

687
00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:33.500
Actually, no.

688
00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:34.620
It's been a long time.

689
00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:36.340
I've been there twice.

690
00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:38.640
We haven't been back in 34 years.

691
00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:39.640
It's time to go back.

692
00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:40.520
I think it's time.

693
00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:42.220
I don't know.

694
00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:43.720
I say don't mess with it.

695
00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:44.540
What?

696
00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:46.340
I say leave it.

697
00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:47.260
Don't revisit.

698
00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:48.740
Never revisit a memory.

699
00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:50.160
Well, it's too good.

700
00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:51.360
It's too good.

701
00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:52.360
You know what I mean?

702
00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:56.700
If you go back and then you're going to be like, oh my gosh, it smells.

703
00:30:57.060 --> 00:30:57.520
What?

704
00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:02.360
I mean, no offense, Venice, but I hear that you're getting floods and maybe it's a little

705
00:31:02.360 --> 00:31:04.980
stinky now and it's a lot of tourists.

706
00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:06.160
Here come the letters.

707
00:31:06.180 --> 00:31:07.480
The cruise ships and the-

708
00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:08.420
All the Venetians.

709
00:31:09.760 --> 00:31:10.460
All the-

710
00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:11.320
I've lost the whole-

711
00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:13.660
The amount of gondoliers that listen to the show.

712
00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:14.920
It's just incredible.

713
00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:16.060
We just lost a few.

714
00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:21.460
Well, I'm just saying, you know, a lot of time has passed and this is an amazing romantic

715
00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:21.980
memory.

716
00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:24.300
Maybe it should just-

717
00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:25.560
I agree with you.

718
00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:25.860
Stay there.

719
00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:26.660
I agree with you.

720
00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:31.660
Maybe in the church and the concert and the rain and all that, but I don't think you need

721
00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:33.280
to rule out all of Venice.

722
00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:36.420
I think there's maybe new places you could discover.

723
00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:36.840
Yeah.

724
00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:37.120
I don't know.

725
00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:38.060
I think leave it.

726
00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:38.700
Okay.

727
00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:40.160
Well, I'm torn now.

728
00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:43.020
Two very different takes on this memory.

729
00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:43.720
Okay.

730
00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:44.600
Last question.

731
00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:45.980
They just asked three.

732
00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:47.260
Last question.

733
00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:50.560
Do you speak any other languages other than English?

734
00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:51.080
Oui.

735
00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:51.840
Oui.

736
00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:52.800
Ho, ho.

737
00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:55.580
I don't, actually.

738
00:31:57.840 --> 00:31:59.520
That was the extent of my French.

739
00:31:59.660 --> 00:31:59.800
No.

740
00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:04.400
I know a little bit of French and a little bit of Spanish, but that's one of the things

741
00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:11.420
I really, really do want to accomplish before I leave this earth is to be able to expand

742
00:32:11.420 --> 00:32:20.280
my awareness and culture and not just be so American-centric, you know, I want to step

743
00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:20.780
out of that.

744
00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:21.750
Yeah.

745
00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:26.620
I spoke recently about not retiring, but taking a break.

746
00:32:27.020 --> 00:32:30.460
For the last 25 years, I've been working really nonstop.

747
00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:30.880
Yeah.

748
00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:35.920
And it's been great, as we know, it's wonderful and, you know, you're supported and you're

749
00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:41.000
having fun and you're engaged and it's artistic and creative and all those wonderful things.

750
00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:43.280
But it's not real life experience.

751
00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:44.040
Yeah.

752
00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:46.520
You're going from one bubble to another bubble to another bubble.

753
00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:53.000
And I'm getting to a point where I feel like I'm a little depleted, like my ideas

754
00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:56.720
aren't as fresh, they're a little harder to come by.

755
00:32:56.720 --> 00:33:01.880
And, ooh, am I being derivative of another character that I've done?

756
00:33:02.020 --> 00:33:03.540
And can I break out of that?

757
00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:09.700
I needed more, I need more influences to come in so that it's more reciprocal.

758
00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:10.380
Yeah.

759
00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:14.080
Right now, there's been a lot of outflow for the last two decades.

760
00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:21.400
And I think I need some real life experience to be able to say, oh, I've never even thought

761
00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:22.080
of that before.

762
00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:27.120
I want to read that classic novel that I've always sworn I was going to read.

763
00:33:27.320 --> 00:33:29.080
I want to learn how to cook.

764
00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:31.320
I want to learn a language.

765
00:33:31.700 --> 00:33:33.960
I want to be in another culture.

766
00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:35.860
And I don't want to talk about show business.

767
00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:42.500
Yeah, we had an amazing conversation with Zach Woods, and we were talking about this exact

768
00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:47.660
thing where we were saying that as artists, you need a life to feed your art.

769
00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:54.460
You can't stay in just an art-making bubble because it's through our observations of other

770
00:33:54.460 --> 00:34:01.680
people and things, and it's through going to that weird cousin's wedding that you get

771
00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:04.240
your ideas and your inspiration.

772
00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:11.720
