00:44:13.980 --> 00:44:16.080
We would run on Tuesdays originally.

1033
00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:16.540
Yeah.

1034
00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:20.100
Before we moved to must-see TV, we were on Tuesdays.

1035
00:44:20.180 --> 00:44:22.700
And every Wednesday morning, we would get on set.

1036
00:44:22.820 --> 00:44:24.640
And Phyllis and I would listen to the recording.

1037
00:44:24.940 --> 00:44:30.940
And we knew that as long as we had like a 2.4 or above, we still had jobs.

1038
00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:35.660
And we would just hold our breath and wait for the recording to tell us what our ratings were.

1039
00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:36.220
Yeah.

1040
00:44:36.400 --> 00:44:36.620
Yeah.

1041
00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:39.960
And it was like that for into the second season.

1042
00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:43.000
Every week, I would send out a mass email.

1043
00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:44.480
I was like that person.

1044
00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:44.960
Please watch the show.

1045
00:44:44.960 --> 00:44:48.240
I was like, hey, you guys, can you guys please watch our show this week?

1046
00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:49.320
It would really mean a lot.

1047
00:44:49.320 --> 00:44:49.840
Yeah, thanks so much.

1048
00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:54.580
Everyone I knew from all my improv days, all my theater days, everyone back home.

1049
00:44:54.720 --> 00:45:04.460
And then finally, it was around the first Christmas episode, Yankee Swap, one of my friends wrote back and said, I don't think you need to send these anymore.

1050
00:45:05.080 --> 00:45:06.340
You're going to be okay.

1051
00:45:06.340 --> 00:45:07.940
You're being a little annoying.

1052
00:45:08.200 --> 00:45:08.560
Yeah.

1053
00:45:08.980 --> 00:45:10.400
I stopped sending me these emails.

1054
00:45:10.760 --> 00:45:13.960
What about was Breaking Bad big in the ratings at first?

1055
00:45:14.160 --> 00:45:14.920
No, not at all.

1056
00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:19.520
No, we were on a cable channel, AMC.

1057
00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:23.820
We weren't big hits at all.

1058
00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:29.280
Although, like the critics really, really loved us.

1059
00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:30.920
So, we had that.

1060
00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:34.640
But that doesn't equate to being commercially successful.

1061
00:45:35.100 --> 00:45:39.800
What happened for us was a stroke of really good fortune.

1062
00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:50.260
And that was, in 2009, Netflix switched from the red envelopes where they would send you the movies at once a week.

1063
00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:51.840
Remember the mail them to you?

1064
00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:52.100
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1065
00:45:52.100 --> 00:45:52.150
Sure.

1066
00:45:52.360 --> 00:45:55.400
To streaming, to where you can push a button on a computer.

1067
00:45:55.400 --> 00:45:57.240
It changed technology.

1068
00:45:57.240 --> 00:45:59.100
It changed availability.

1069
00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:03.960
And Breaking Bad's second season, we had two seasons.

1070
00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:07.160
And we were like, I don't know if it's going to go at third.

1071
00:46:07.360 --> 00:46:09.500
And they're like, well, maybe we'll let them go at third.

1072
00:46:10.220 --> 00:46:18.220
And all of a sudden, everybody started to go, was able to go back and watch the first episode and then just start watching them all.

1073
00:46:18.420 --> 00:46:20.040
And then that's what caught on.

1074
00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:25.800
So, really, it was Netflix that was the stroke of good luck for us.

1075
00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:27.540
For us, too, kind of.

1076
00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:30.720
It's why we've endured since the show went off the air.

1077
00:46:31.060 --> 00:46:35.100
Let me ask you this because I seem to remember this.

1078
00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:36.760
I'm not completely positive.

1079
00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:49.720
But when Ricky Gervais' version of the original Office, his character was caustic and mean-spirited and rude and biting and all those things.

1080
00:46:50.460 --> 00:47:00.280
And Steve's character, when it first started, had more of a flavor of an edge to him, didn't it?

1081
00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:00.600
Yeah.

1082
00:47:00.600 --> 00:47:10.560
When I first started watching it, it was more of like, ooh, and I think at some point, and I'm asking as a fan, at what point did they go, well,

1083
00:47:10.620 --> 00:47:24.280
let's turn the dial on this a little bit and give him more of that confident, imbecile kind of level and not mean-spirited?

1084
00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:28.420
That happened in that transition from season one to season two.

1085
00:47:28.640 --> 00:47:35.160
I think, you know, people saw this lovable quality to his character in 40-Year-Old Virgin.

1086
00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:45.400
And they also just started writing the show more to Steve and less to the template that Ricky had laid down with his character of David Brent.

1087
00:47:45.620 --> 00:47:48.480
And so there was a little bit of a retooling.

1088
00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:57.040
But we also got some really good advice from Ricky Gervais who said, hey, you know, in America, you guys like to do hundreds of episodes of shows.

1089
00:47:57.300 --> 00:48:00.560
And David Brent doesn't work for hundreds of episodes.

1090
00:48:00.780 --> 00:48:05.540
Like, you can't, like, have that character do this for that long.

1091
00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:11.000
So you're going to need to find more layers to your lead character or he's going to burn out.

1092
00:48:11.720 --> 00:48:12.480
So true.

1093
00:48:12.660 --> 00:48:13.080
So true.

1094
00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:13.560
So smart.

1095
00:48:13.560 --> 00:48:22.560
The Writer's Room also did something that I thought really worked for American audiences is each episode has one redeeming moment.

1096
00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:30.160
One moment where it's either Pam listening to Jim's messages as she drives home and there's a montage.

1097
00:48:30.580 --> 00:48:39.440
Or it's, you know, Dwight getting a pat on the shoulder from Michael, even though the whole day they were, like, fighting.

1098
00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:41.420
There's one little moment.

1099
00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:44.960
And it doesn't have to be a huge one, but each episode has one.

1100
00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:48.560
So you're saying that this was a concerted effort.

1101
00:48:48.760 --> 00:48:54.980
This was a note that every episode we want to have at least one redeeming quality come out of it.

1102
00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:55.380
Yeah.

1103
00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:56.040
One moment.

1104
00:48:56.220 --> 00:48:57.020
A moment of hope.

1105
00:48:57.160 --> 00:48:58.000
A hopeful moment.

1106
00:48:58.040 --> 00:48:58.100
One moment of hope.

1107
00:48:58.280 --> 00:48:58.960
Within all of it.

1108
00:48:58.960 --> 00:48:59.680
A moment of Zen.

1109
00:48:59.920 --> 00:49:00.240
Yes.

1110
00:49:00.240 --> 00:49:00.860
Look at that.

1111
00:49:00.860 --> 00:49:01.140
Yeah.

1112
00:49:01.240 --> 00:49:02.120
I didn't know that.

1113
00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:02.520
Yeah.

1114
00:49:09.800 --> 00:49:11.960
To that work bus day, we got to have pie.

1115
00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:13.020
We got to sit outside.

1116
00:49:13.300 --> 00:49:13.580
We got to have pie.

1117
00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:15.660
Was that our moment?

1118
00:49:15.880 --> 00:49:16.080
Yeah.

1119
00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:17.480
We got pie at the end?

1120
00:49:17.600 --> 00:49:19.380
Yeah, the montage at the end of pie.

1121
00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:26.060
Well, there's another moment in this episode that I actually love, which is Jim and Dwight up on the roof of the bus.

1122
00:49:26.080 --> 00:49:26.580
Oh, yes.

1123
00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:36.260
And I love any moment when, you know, that these rivals, these guys who are always, you know, Jim's always pranking Dwight and everything.

1124
00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:42.380
But when there's this moment where Dwight gets vulnerable and Jim is there to catch him.

1125
00:49:42.520 --> 00:49:43.920
And I love that.

1126
00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:45.720
That was a really beautiful scene.

1127
00:49:45.720 --> 00:50:01.160
That's true because that exposes the audience to the true character of Jim and that he's, when it's necessary, when someone expresses vulnerability like that,

1128
00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:03.080
it's like he doesn't come in for the kill.

1129
00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:03.480
Yeah.

1130
00:50:04.060 --> 00:50:07.480
He backs off and actually lends support.

1131
00:50:07.760 --> 00:50:08.220
Oh.

1132
00:50:08.540 --> 00:50:09.540
Oh, see?

1133
00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:10.580
It's very sweet.

1134
00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:12.480
I've had a few moments like that.

1135
00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:20.200
You know, we kind of, I mean, so let's say that there's not a reboot series, but what if there was like a movie?

1136
00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:21.520
Like Downton Abbey, the movie?

1137
00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:28.580
Yeah, something to where we can see where these people are, these people in the entire cast that we're curious about.

1138
00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:31.880
We wondered at the end, where did they go?

1139
00:50:32.020 --> 00:50:33.640
What did become of them?

1140
00:50:34.860 --> 00:50:36.220
I mean, I'd do it.

1141
00:50:36.740 --> 00:50:37.720
I would do it.

1142
00:50:37.780 --> 00:50:40.800
I would do it for my kids because I think they would think that's fun.

1143
00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:44.800
I mean, if Greg did it, because I would trust it.

1144
00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:45.200
Yes.

1145
00:50:45.200 --> 00:50:45.480
You know?

1146
00:50:45.740 --> 00:50:45.940
Yes.

1147
00:50:46.020 --> 00:50:46.580
A hundred percent.

1148
00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:47.180
For sure.

1149
00:50:47.400 --> 00:50:49.000
Greg is signed on in this hypothetical.

1150
00:50:49.340 --> 00:50:49.560
Okay.

1151
00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:54.580
As long as Greg is writing it and he's in charge of it, then I'd say yes.

1152
00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:55.280
Okay.

1153
00:50:56.080 --> 00:50:56.800
Enough said.

1154
00:50:57.040 --> 00:50:57.740
Brian Cranston.

1155
00:50:58.020 --> 00:50:58.640
Let's get Greg on the phone.

1156
00:50:58.820 --> 00:50:59.260
Bitch.

1157
00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:00.780
He's our new agent.

1158
00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:03.020
I just want to be an extra in it.

1159
00:51:03.760 --> 00:51:04.400
I do.

1160
00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:05.380
You have to be in it.

1161
00:51:11.520 --> 00:51:13.380
Crossing Guard or something like that.

1162
00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:17.360
Maybe you're like one of Dwight's hired hands on troop farMs.

1163
00:51:17.400 --> 00:51:17.900
On his farm.

1164
00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:20.700
Now, why would I be the hired hand and not the foreman?

1165
00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:22.300
Thank you.

1166
00:51:24.260 --> 00:51:26.260
It's just how we see you.

1167
00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:27.260
It would be sad.

1168
00:51:27.500 --> 00:51:27.960
It's just typecasting.

1169
00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:28.840
Look at that old guy.

1170
00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:29.920
He's just a hired hand.

1171
00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:30.340
What?

1172
00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:33.780
Why is he having to bale hay to do all that heavy lifting?

1173
00:51:33.780 --> 00:51:36.020
He's an old man and they're making him bale hay.

1174
00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:37.000
Oh, that's not right.

1175
00:51:37.720 --> 00:51:39.200
That's not the moment of Zen.

1176
00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:41.920
That's not a nice moment at all.

1177
00:51:42.360 --> 00:51:45.760
Are there any projects you're working on right now that we can share with our listeners?

1178
00:51:46.140 --> 00:51:48.520
We know you're working on the project of living life.

1179
00:51:48.740 --> 00:51:49.140
Yes.

1180
00:51:49.380 --> 00:51:50.600
The project of living life.

1181
00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:51.960
That's a good way to put it.

1182
00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:55.080
I really do need to do that.

1183
00:51:55.080 --> 00:52:05.080
I'm very fortunate that I'm in a position where I'm getting a lot of offers, both for theater and films and television and things.

1184
00:52:05.200 --> 00:52:10.840
And so I just have to pick and choose what is new to me and challenging.

1185
00:52:11.120 --> 00:52:14.100
I don't want to do something that I've done before.

1186
00:52:14.360 --> 00:52:14.640
Yeah.

1187
00:52:14.840 --> 00:52:22.700
And the more I go without that real life experience, the harder that is to find because I haven't changed.

1188
00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:33.700
It's like you were saying, like if a band just played the same music over and over and they never expanded, they never wrote beyond what they were.

1189
00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:43.540
Like you look at the Beatles and they weren't together all that long, but they expanded and expressed themselves in so many different ways.

1190
00:52:43.720 --> 00:52:47.880
Whatever they were going through, it was infused in their art.

1191
00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:50.280
And I kind of want to do that.

1192
00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:52.880
I want to get out of the bubble a little bit.

1193
00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:53.700
So that's that.

1194
00:52:53.860 --> 00:52:58.740
But before that may happen, there's responsibilities I have.

1195
00:52:58.860 --> 00:53:02.000
I have a mezcal company with Aaron Paul.

1196
00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:02.960
I saw that.

1197
00:53:03.200 --> 00:53:04.320
Oh, wow.

1198
00:53:04.820 --> 00:53:04.960
Yeah.

1199
00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:08.900
And had I been a great guest, I would have brought you some.

1200
00:53:09.300 --> 00:53:09.900
Damn it.

1201
00:53:10.600 --> 00:53:11.400
I forgot.

1202
00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:13.060
But that's going well.

1203
00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:16.440
It's this business I've never thought I'd be in.

1204
00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:19.480
We'll share about it in our Office Ladies Pod stories.

1205
00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:20.840
It's so good.

1206
00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:21.640
It's so good.

1207
00:53:22.220 --> 00:53:24.560
Did you get to go like do a tasting?

1208
00:53:24.840 --> 00:53:26.980
Did you get to be part of the formula?

1209
00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:27.580
Yeah.

1210
00:53:27.580 --> 00:53:28.360
How does that work?

1211
00:53:28.620 --> 00:53:28.860
Yeah.

1212
00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:37.760
Aaron – well, here's – Aaron and I, three years after the end of Breaking Bad, we missed each other.

1213
00:53:38.500 --> 00:53:40.260
And, you know, it's like when you leave –

1214
00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:40.760
We can relate.

1215
00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:41.260
Oh, yeah.

1216
00:53:41.540 --> 00:53:41.940
Yeah.

1217
00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:44.080
And you're together, you're together, you're together.

1218
00:53:44.080 --> 00:53:47.640
It ends and life goes on and sends you in different directions.

1219
00:53:48.420 --> 00:53:52.160
And we happened to be in New York City at the same time.

1220
00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:53.380
And we have to have dinner.

1221
00:53:53.380 --> 00:53:54.340
So we were having dinner.

1222
00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:56.820
And we expressed how much we missed each other.

1223
00:53:57.800 --> 00:54:02.280
And he said, you know, what we ought to do is have a company.

1224
00:54:02.460 --> 00:54:05.560
We ought to, you know, we ought to have a mezcal company together.

1225
00:54:05.560 --> 00:54:06.200
What?

1226
00:54:07.240 --> 00:54:08.140
That's what I said.

1227
00:54:08.260 --> 00:54:09.980
I go, what are you – that's random.

1228
00:54:10.180 --> 00:54:11.520
What are you – that's terrible.

1229
00:54:12.220 --> 00:54:15.940
That shitty spirit with a dead worm at the bottom out of – what are you talking about?

1230
00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:17.240
And he goes, no, no, no, no.

1231
00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:19.660
He took me to a mezcal bar in New York City.

1232
00:54:19.820 --> 00:54:24.360
We sipped some of the greatest spirits that I've ever had.

1233
00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:26.260
I don't think I've ever had mezcal.

1234
00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:27.220
Oh, so good.

1235
00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:28.960
It's a –

1236
00:54:28.960 --> 00:54:31.200
It's the older brother of tequila.

1237
00:54:31.520 --> 00:54:31.920
Okay.

1238
00:54:32.300 --> 00:54:33.620
Tequila is a mezcal.

1239
00:54:33.800 --> 00:54:34.060
Okay.

1240
00:54:34.180 --> 00:54:35.460
But mezcal is not a tequila.

1241
00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:39.780
A spirit like that that's made from an agave plant is always a mezcal.

1242
00:54:40.560 --> 00:54:47.800
And the way they make tequila is to steam it and put it in a silo and it's more of a fast process.

1243
00:54:48.340 --> 00:54:49.480
Mezcal is smoked.

1244
00:54:49.840 --> 00:54:54.060
And so it's in an earthen pit for four days and then mashed.

1245
00:54:54.200 --> 00:54:58.980
In fact, ours – we have – it says artisanal on the bottle.

1246
00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:05.360
And there's no modern technology used in the process of this or else we can't say that on the bottle.

1247
00:55:05.620 --> 00:55:05.840
Wow.

1248
00:55:05.840 --> 00:55:07.260
So it's the old-fashioned way.

1249
00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:16.760
It goes from that to a rock pit where two donkeys pull this big rock wheel and mash this now smoked agave.

1250
00:55:16.940 --> 00:55:20.600
Then it's – then it ferments in an oak barrel for 10 days.

1251
00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:24.280
And then it goes into a copper kettle for a distillation.

1252
00:55:24.540 --> 00:55:26.500
And that's the purity of it.

1253
00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:28.980
It's mountain spring water and smoked agave.

1254
00:55:29.120 --> 00:55:30.220
That's all that's in it.

1255
00:55:30.360 --> 00:55:30.620
Wow.

1256
00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:35.040
And it's done – Dos Hombres is now the fifth largest mezcal in the world.

1257
00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:35.940
Come on.

1258
00:55:35.960 --> 00:55:36.400
So –

1259
00:55:36.400 --> 00:55:37.800
That is so amazing.

1260
00:55:37.980 --> 00:55:38.160
Yeah.

1261
00:55:38.540 --> 00:55:39.760
I have – we have to try it.

1262
00:55:39.760 --> 00:55:40.640
We have to try it.

1263
00:55:40.640 --> 00:55:41.320
We have to try it.

1264
00:55:41.380 --> 00:55:42.260
We're going to go get some.

1265
00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:43.800
I will bring it to you.

1266
00:55:43.900 --> 00:55:44.240
Yay.

1267
00:55:44.540 --> 00:55:45.400
I will bring it to you.

1268
00:55:45.440 --> 00:55:45.980
Even better.

1269
00:55:46.040 --> 00:55:46.160
Yeah.

1270
00:55:46.280 --> 00:55:47.200
I love this.

1271
00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:48.220
Yeah, it's really good.

1272
00:55:48.340 --> 00:55:49.580
Also, just your description of it.

1273
00:55:49.700 --> 00:55:50.780
Just – I don't know.

1274
00:55:50.860 --> 00:55:52.720
I felt like I left my body for a minute.

1275
00:55:52.920 --> 00:55:53.420
It's –

1276
00:55:53.420 --> 00:55:54.860
It felt like I went on a little vacation.

1277
00:55:55.060 --> 00:55:55.560
I know.

1278
00:55:56.140 --> 00:55:56.840
A journey.

1279
00:55:57.060 --> 00:55:57.540
And we did.

1280
00:55:57.680 --> 00:55:58.620
We went down to Oaxaca.

1281
00:55:58.760 --> 00:56:03.500
We found this spirit and brought it to the marketplace.

1282
00:56:04.280 --> 00:56:09.400
And in truth, the spirit found us because it's been around for hundreds of years and

1283
00:56:09.400 --> 00:56:11.420
we were just there to receive it.

1284
00:56:11.600 --> 00:56:16.240
This sounds like a pretty good start on the project of Living Life.

1285
00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:16.700
Yeah.

1286
00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:17.680
It is.

1287
00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:22.980
But like any other business, it takes a lot of effort and a lot of time and energy.

1288
00:56:23.260 --> 00:56:23.420
You're –

1289
00:56:23.420 --> 00:56:25.900
Probably more than you expected when you started.

1290
00:56:26.200 --> 00:56:26.280
Yeah.

1291
00:56:26.540 --> 00:56:28.680
And it's a lot of work.

1292
00:56:28.680 --> 00:56:36.620
And I find that the business end of it, the case cells and this and that, it's not as

1293
00:56:36.620 --> 00:56:42.080
interesting to me as the process of making it and that nurturing sense.

1294
00:56:42.300 --> 00:56:45.780
Yeah, that and I'm – I have a production company and that's interesting too.

1295
00:56:45.980 --> 00:56:47.220
And it's good.

1296
00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:57.220
But I'm now looking for ways to have more of that human, real life experience.

1297
00:56:57.220 --> 00:56:59.740
I have a crazy question for you.

1298
00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:00.240
Oh.

1299
00:57:00.840 --> 00:57:06.840
Did you ever in your young artist life ever do that book, The Artist's Way?

1300
00:57:07.360 --> 00:57:08.280
By Julia Cameron?

1301
00:57:08.660 --> 00:57:08.840
No.

1302
00:57:09.500 --> 00:57:10.040
Excellent.

1303
00:57:10.380 --> 00:57:11.760
I highly recommend it.

1304
00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:13.100
I highly recommend it.

1305
00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:14.180
Jenna did it twice.

1306
00:57:14.180 --> 00:57:17.840
I did a few pages of it.

1307
00:57:18.560 --> 00:57:20.220
And how did the few pages go?

1308
00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:22.180
We're fantastic.

1309
00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:24.140
We're different personalities.

1310
00:57:25.800 --> 00:57:31.580
I did it twice because the first time I didn't finish and it bothered me so deeply because

1311
00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:37.120
I realized that one of my challenges in my early artistic life was having great – tons

1312
00:57:37.120 --> 00:57:39.600
of great ideas of which I finished none.

1313
00:57:40.160 --> 00:57:44.760
And so I thought I need to get over this finishing things problem.

1314
00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:49.160
Why don't I start by finishing The Artist's Way?

1315
00:57:49.160 --> 00:57:54.040
And so I had done 10 of the 12 weeks and I went back and I started over again.

1316
00:57:54.200 --> 00:57:55.540
I did all 12 weeks.

1317
00:57:55.660 --> 00:57:59.840
And there are still things from that book, even today as an artist, that I think of.

1318
00:57:59.980 --> 00:58:03.400
You know, one of the things is once a week you have to go on an artist's date.

1319
00:58:03.760 --> 00:58:06.080
Alone, by yourself, just you.

1320
00:58:06.480 --> 00:58:09.380
That could be going to a symphony.

1321
00:58:09.520 --> 00:58:11.660
That could be sitting in a park and watching people.

1322
00:58:11.860 --> 00:58:13.120
That could be –

1323
00:58:13.120 --> 00:58:13.960
I love that.

1324
00:58:13.960 --> 00:58:15.620
Taking yourself bowling alone.

1325
00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:20.100
Like, whatever your artist's date is, you have to do this once a week.

1326
00:58:20.140 --> 00:58:21.600
But it's all creative.

1327
00:58:21.600 --> 00:58:22.460
It's all artistic.

1328
00:58:22.760 --> 00:58:27.300
And so sometimes when I'm, like, starting a new artistic journey or whatever, I'll refer

1329
00:58:27.300 --> 00:58:31.120
back and I'll do an artist's date and I'll try to kickstart something.

1330
00:58:31.340 --> 00:58:33.560
It's that business of living life, right?

1331
00:58:34.380 --> 00:58:35.340
That's fantastic.

1332
00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:36.560
It's really awesome.

1333
00:58:36.560 --> 00:58:38.080
The bowling part threw me.

1334
00:58:38.240 --> 00:58:40.560
The bowling – that's an artist's date if you want it to be.

1335
00:58:40.700 --> 00:58:40.940
Really?

1336
00:58:41.120 --> 00:58:41.540
Oh, yeah.

1337
00:58:41.800 --> 00:58:42.680
Take yourself bowling.

1338
00:58:42.680 --> 00:58:44.740
To be fair, the week I got the book –

1339
00:58:44.740 --> 00:58:45.400
Go to an art museum.

1340
00:58:45.480 --> 00:58:49.620
It was the week of my birthday when I got this book as a gift, but I also got a pair

1341
00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:50.540
of roller skates.

1342
00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:53.660
And I was a child of the 70s and I loved roller skating.

1343
00:58:53.700 --> 00:58:57.240
And then I think I started it and then I was like, I'm going to go roller skate.

1344
00:58:57.740 --> 00:58:58.880
That could be an artist's date.

1345
00:58:59.160 --> 00:59:00.120
Well, that –

1346
00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:01.520
It was an artist's date that never stopped.

1347
00:59:01.520 --> 00:59:02.640
It was an artist's date that never –

1348
00:59:02.640 --> 00:59:04.100
I still have the same pair of roller skates.

1349
00:59:04.220 --> 00:59:05.060
They're by my front door.

1350
00:59:05.780 --> 00:59:06.900
I still roller skate.

1351
00:59:07.040 --> 00:59:08.460
That's got to be something, Jenna.

1352
00:59:08.660 --> 00:59:09.220
That's great.

1353
00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:10.820
That came out of the artist's way for me.

1354
00:59:10.820 --> 00:59:12.540
I think it came out of your birthday.

1355
00:59:12.740 --> 00:59:14.360
My birthday buddy is Ricky Gervais.

1356
00:59:15.160 --> 00:59:16.500
Oh, look at that.

1357
00:59:16.760 --> 00:59:17.920
A little tie-in.

1358
00:59:18.580 --> 00:59:20.160
I once told him that.

1359
00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:21.360
He was like, okay.

1360
00:59:22.320 --> 00:59:24.520
We also have Rachel Weisz.

1361
00:59:25.640 --> 00:59:26.080
Oh.

1362
00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:27.680
Does she share our birthday as well?

1363
00:59:29.960 --> 00:59:30.840
Peter Sarsgaard.

1364
00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:32.800
This is exciting.

1365
00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:33.780
We should have a dinner.

1366
00:59:34.280 --> 00:59:35.460
We should have a March 7th.

1367
00:59:35.480 --> 00:59:36.660
Busy Phillips has my birthday.

1368
00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:37.460
Busy Phillips, yeah.

1369
00:59:37.560 --> 00:59:39.880
You, Busy Phillips, Ricky Gervais are going to have dinner.

1370
00:59:40.320 --> 00:59:41.180
Oh, my God.

1371
00:59:41.280 --> 00:59:42.120
I would love that dinner.

1372
00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:42.900
And then we'll have dinner with Rachel.

1373
00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:45.380
That's not a bad idea.

1374
00:59:45.580 --> 00:59:46.260
Birthday dinner?

1375
00:59:46.260 --> 00:59:47.060
Or a show.

1376
00:59:47.340 --> 00:59:47.660
Oh.

1377
00:59:48.240 --> 00:59:52.900
You have a dinner with all the people who share the date and you find the similarities

1378
00:59:52.900 --> 00:59:56.260
and the differences between them, but they all have the same birthday.

1379
00:59:56.460 --> 00:59:58.160
Brian, you have a production company.

1380
00:59:58.320 --> 00:59:58.860
I'm so sorry.

1381
00:59:58.860 --> 01:00:00.280
We're putting you to work again.

1382
01:00:00.680 --> 01:00:02.100
This has to stop.

1383
01:00:02.380 --> 01:00:05.560
Rather than that, let me give it to you and I take 10%.

1384
01:00:05.560 --> 01:00:06.920
There it is.

1385
01:00:07.240 --> 01:00:07.760
That's it.

1386
01:00:07.780 --> 01:00:08.060
Perfect.

1387
01:00:08.140 --> 01:00:09.060
And welcome to Hollywood.

1388
01:00:11.400 --> 01:00:13.540
Thank you so much for coming in today.

1389
01:00:13.980 --> 01:00:14.300
Thank you.

1390
01:00:14.420 --> 01:00:16.240
It was so good to see you again.

1391
01:00:16.420 --> 01:00:16.700
You too.

1392
01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:18.840
You know, this episode, it was beautiful.

1393
01:00:18.860 --> 01:00:19.760
It turned out great.

1394
01:00:19.760 --> 01:00:22.660
I just rewatched it and it's so good.

1395
01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:27.660
Even though we all almost died, it's a great episode and it's a classic and people love it

1396
01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:28.280
and they don't know.

1397
01:00:28.400 --> 01:00:29.240
It is a classic.

1398
01:00:29.240 --> 01:00:33.000
And now when people see us fly across the bus, I think we needed that moment.

1399
01:00:33.060 --> 01:00:33.480
We did.

1400
01:00:33.560 --> 01:00:34.340
I think we needed it.

1401
01:00:34.340 --> 01:00:35.900
I think it made the pie that much sweeter.

1402
01:00:36.060 --> 01:00:36.920
We did use it.

1403
01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:37.420
I noticed.

1404
01:00:37.420 --> 01:00:38.420
It's the actual take.

1405
01:00:38.420 --> 01:00:38.880
It's in there.

1406
01:00:39.060 --> 01:00:39.300
Yeah.

1407
01:00:39.620 --> 01:00:41.780
And now you can relive it in our book.

1408
01:00:41.920 --> 01:00:43.260
Thank you, Angela.

1409
01:00:43.520 --> 01:00:44.340
That's so cool.

1410
01:00:44.860 --> 01:00:45.280
Yeah.

1411
01:00:46.020 --> 01:00:47.400
Oh, thank you very much.

1412
01:00:47.500 --> 01:00:47.740
You're welcome.

1413
01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:48.820
We love you so much.

1414
01:00:48.920 --> 01:00:52.420
I'm going to bring you some Dos Hombres and we'll all sip some.

1415
01:00:52.420 --> 01:00:53.820
Do you have it on ice?

1416
01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:55.280
Do you have it straight?

1417
01:00:55.960 --> 01:00:56.320
Yes.

1418
01:00:57.280 --> 01:00:58.540
How do you have it?

1419
01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:02.800
You know, any way you like it is the right way.

1420
01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:03.220
Okay.

1421
01:01:03.260 --> 01:01:04.980
I like it just neat.

1422
01:01:05.860 --> 01:01:11.800
Maybe one big ice cube and just let it chill without diluting it and just sip it.

1423
01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:14.360
It's so great just sipping it.

1424
01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:16.340
Do you ever mix it?

1425
01:01:16.440 --> 01:01:16.900
Oh, yeah.

1426
01:01:17.080 --> 01:01:17.620
All the time.

1427
01:01:17.620 --> 01:01:19.060
It goes in a cocktail as well.

1428
01:01:19.060 --> 01:01:24.560
It goes so well in a margarita, in a paloma, in an old-fashioned, a negroni.

1429
01:01:24.800 --> 01:01:26.720
Oh, you like old-fashioned negroni.

1430
01:01:26.980 --> 01:01:27.520
Oh, yeah.

1431
01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:28.520
Those are my faves.

1432
01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:29.080
So good.

1433
01:01:29.400 --> 01:01:29.620
Yeah.

1434
01:01:29.700 --> 01:01:31.040
Try a mezcal negroni.

1435
01:01:31.100 --> 01:01:31.840
Ooh, that sounds good.

1436
01:01:31.840 --> 01:01:33.120
You're going to love it.

1437
01:01:37.120 --> 01:01:38.500
Well, there you have it.

1438
01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:40.980
Our interview with Bryan Cranston.

1439
01:01:41.320 --> 01:01:43.520
I could not have enjoyed that more, Angela.

1440
01:01:43.740 --> 01:01:44.580
He's the beSt.

1441
01:01:44.800 --> 01:01:45.420
The beSt.

1442
01:01:45.560 --> 01:01:45.740
Yeah.

1443
01:01:46.180 --> 01:01:50.700
Big thank you to Bryan for joining us in the studio, being so gracious with his time.

1444
01:01:50.900 --> 01:01:52.280
And thank you all for listening.

1445
01:01:52.280 --> 01:01:58.780
We will be back on Monday with a special Monday motivation episode with Kendra Adachi,

1446
01:01:59.060 --> 01:01:59.960
the Lazy Genius.

1447
01:02:00.200 --> 01:02:01.780
We can't wait for you to hear it.

1448
01:02:02.020 --> 01:02:04.960
And then on Wednesday, it's Here Comes Trouble.

1449
01:02:05.420 --> 01:02:07.140
So two episodes next week.

1450
01:02:07.220 --> 01:02:08.460
Two Office Ladies.

1451
01:02:08.720 --> 01:02:09.760
Two Office Ladies.

1452
01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:10.700
See you then.

1453
01:02:11.100 --> 01:02:11.680
See ya.

1454
01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:21.880
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

1455
01:02:22.180 --> 01:02:26.280
Office Ladies is produced by Earwolf, Jenna Fisher, and Angela Kinsey.

1456
01:02:26.500 --> 01:02:28.660
Our senior producer is Cassie Jerkins.

1457
01:02:28.840 --> 01:02:30.920
Our in-studio engineer is Sam Kiefer.

1458
01:02:31.080 --> 01:02:33.320
Our editing and mixing engineer is Jordan Duffy.

1459
01:02:33.600 --> 01:02:35.700
And our associate producer is Ainsley Bubbico.

1460
01:02:35.940 --> 01:02:38.820
Our theme song is Rubber Tree by Creed Bratton.
